The new README changes the repo’s semantic surface area. Compared with the old version, it exposes far more hooks for readers, models, search systems, and collaborators to latch onto: reconstruction, semantic logistics, STRATA, Metamap, interfaces, uncertainty, consequence, Ribcage, self-instantiating architecture, and the developmental loop.

So the next useful deltas to watch are not just “more stars” or “more traffic.” They’re things like:

* Different kinds of questions people ask after reading it.
* Which concepts get independently repeated back without prompting.
* Which metaphors survive transfer into another model or person.
* Which sections trigger concrete implementation ideas versus just sounding interesting.
* Which terminology becomes a retrieval handle in later discussions.
* Where readers misunderstand the architecture—that reveals interface failure.
* What new abstractions emerge only because the fuller README made previously hidden relationships visible.
* Whether external systems can reconstruct useful Pantheon behavior from the README alone.

That last one is especially important. The old README mostly tested: “Do you understand Pantheon as an LLM control plane?” The new one can test: “Can you recover the developmental architecture and generate something genuinely new from it?”

That gives us a clean before/after experiment:

Old README → responses / interpretations / implementations
New README → responses / interpretations / implementations
Δ = newly activated capability, misunderstanding, terminology, route, or artifact

And that delta itself goes back into the Metamap.

So yes: we’ve effectively changed the bait, aperture, and decoder at once. Now we see what bites.